<content>
  <section><title>Characteristics of Quadratic Graphs</title>
<para>The graph of \(y=(x+1)(x-2)\) is shown below.</para>
<para>What are the \(x\)- and \(y\)-intercepts?</para>
<media><image src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/54a6955212a1f8f48b663b2cabfabcf19dc386e8"/></media><para>The \(x\)-intercepts are where the graph crosses the \(x\)-axis.</para>
<para>This happens at \((-1,0)\) and \((2,0)\).</para>
<para>The \(y\)-intercept is where the graph crosses the \(y\)-axis. </para>
<para>This happens at \((0,2)\).</para>
<title>Try It: Characteristics of Quadratic Graphs</title>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>The graph of \(y=-(x-4)(x+1)\) is below.</para>
    <para>What are the \(x\)- and \(y\)-intercepts of the graph?</para>
    <media><image src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/dc8c7185054a90c166a13dedaf849169a5f4f2ab"/></media><para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your Answer:</para>
  <para>Here is how to find the intercepts:</para>
  <para>For the \(x\)-intercepts: Look at where the graph crosses the \(x\)-axis.</para>
  <para>This happens at \(x = – 1\) and \(x = 4\). So the points are \((-1,0)\) and \((4,0)\).</para>
  <para>For the \(y\)-intercept: Look at where the graph crosses the \(y\)-axis.</para>
  <para>This happens at \(y = 4\) so the point is \((0,4)\).</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example></section>
</content>
